  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 153 AM EDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis:\n\nSeasonally impressive troughing digging across the Great Lakes \ntonight will punch east of the region today, placing us underneath \nnorthwest mid/upper-level flow through most of this week and perhaps \ninto this weekend. The strong cyclone supported by aforementioned \ntroughing will continue to track across Ontario into Quebec today \nwith a secondary cold front expected to swing across northern \nMichigan later this afternoon and evening. Surface high pressure is \nexpected to build across the northern Plains and Upper Midwest \nheading into the middle of the week underneath subsidence aloft, \neventually centering itself over the Great Lakes for the second half \nof the week. Additional troughing progressing over the region may \nbring a more active pattern this weekend.\n\nForecast Details:\n\nOngoing storms across northern lower Michigan will continue to track \nsoutheast over the next few hours. At this point, lightning and \nlocally heavy rainfall will be the primary hazards and additional \nsevere weather is not anticipated. \n\nAfter tonight's storms track southeast of the area, a short break in \nprecip chances is anticipated through mid-morning. Additional \nscattered showers may pop up along and ahead of the previously \nmentioned cold front in the presence of ~500-1,000 J/kg MLCAPE -- \nenough to support at least isolated thunder chances into the \nafternoon. Thunder chances look to be cut off later in the afternoon \nafter the front passes with temperatures cooling into the 60s for \nmuch of the area. Ample low-level saturation may support light \ndrizzle/mist across parts of the area this evening into tonight with \ngusty northwest winds off of Lake Michigan/Lake Superior. Winds \ncould gust as high as 30-40 mph this afternoon into tonight, leading \nto high swim risks being in place across many northern Michigan \nbeaches due to the threat of high waves and dangerous currents. \nLooking ahead, cool temperatures in the 60s and low 70s are expected \nfor most areas on Wednesday before warming back close to seasonal \naverages through the second half of the week with high pressure \noverhead. Rain chances look to hold off Wednesday through Friday \nbefore returning to the forecast later this weekend.",
